Reproduction: withBusiestCompany's transaction on plain `BEGIN` (READ COMMITTED),
against the disposable fixture isaflow-fixture-r165-goal (127.0.0.1:55485), 3
consecutive runs of just the new regression test:

$ DATABASE_URL="postgres://postgres:***@127.0.0.1:55485/isaflow_schema_fixture" \
  DATABASE_SSL=disable bunx vitest run \
  tests/unit/mcp/w2/generalLedgerStream.integration.test.ts -t "R-165 regression"

Run 1/3:
 FAIL  tests/unit/mcp/w2/generalLedgerStream.integration.test.ts > general-ledger line stream — real database > is not disturbed by a peer session committing a posted line mid-read (R-165 regression)
AssertionError: expected 762 to be 760 // Object.is equality
- Expected
+ Received
- 760
+ 762
 ❯ tests/unit/mcp/w2/generalLedgerStream.integration.test.ts:171:35
     expect(whole.lineCount).toBe(before.lineCount);
 ❯ withBusiestCompany tests/unit/mcp/w2/generalLedgerStream.integration.test.ts:61:5

Run 2/3: identical failure, same assertion, same values (762 vs 760).
Run 3/3: identical failure, same assertion, same values (762 vs 760).

Mechanism: 760 seeded posted lines. The regression test reads a page
(`before`, taken before the concurrent write), then a SEPARATE pg.Pool
connection commits one balanced posted entry (2 lines) for the same
company, then the test reads the whole period (`whole`). Under plain BEGIN
(READ COMMITTED), each statement in the open transaction gets its own
snapshot, so `whole` — a LATER statement — sees the peer's commit
(760 + 2 = 762) while `before` — an EARLIER statement — does not. This is
the same shape as the originally reported flake: a 115 diff between two
reads taken moments apart, caused by a peer's write landing between them.

Restored the fix (BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ) from a saved copy
of the file (not `git checkout`) after this check; see ci.log for the full
suite green afterward.

